## The right tool for the job

<At levels="simple">

You've now seen what a language model does: it writes the likely next token, again and again, using
only what's in its prompt and what training left in its weights. That tells you when it will help.

It helps most with writing. In one experiment, 453 professionals did writing tasks from their own
jobs, and half of them could use ChatGPT. Those who could finished 40% faster, and their work was
rated 18% better.<Cite id="noy2023" /> For other jobs, a calculator, a search, or an expert does
better. Four questions tell them apart.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Everything so far comes down to one fact: a language model writes the likely next token, again and
again, from what's in its prompt and what training left in its weights. That fact tells you when to
reach for one, and when not to.

Writing is where it shines. In a preregistered experiment, 453 college-educated professionals did
writing tasks from their own occupations, and half of them, chosen at random, could use ChatGPT.
They took 40% less time, and graders rated their work 18% better.<Cite id="noy2023" /> Other jobs go
the other way, and four questions, each from an earlier chapter, tell them apart.

</At>

## Four questions

<At levels="simple">

**Would a likely-sounding answer be a good one?** For rewording, summing up, drafting, and
brainstorming, yes: you want text that reads well. For a sum or a date, you want one exact answer,
and the likely-looking one can be wrong. Without a calculator, the model in <ChapterRef to="tools" /> worked out
1234 × 5678 step by step and got 7,050,952. The answer is 7,006,652.

**Can you give it everything it needs to know?** It knows only its prompt and its training: nothing
about your files, nothing after its cutoff, and little about rare things. Asked ten times who wrote a
novel made up for this site, it named nine different authors (<ChapterRef to="making-things-up" />). Paste the facts in, or
let the app search for them (<ChapterRef to="search-and-memory" />).

**Can you check the result quickly, and live with a mistake?** The model sounds just as sure when
it's wrong. Asked ten times who won the 1987 Tour de France, it said Eddy Merckx every time; it was
Stephen Roche. So a model saves time only when checking its work is quicker than doing the job
yourself. For anything that matters, have someone who knows check it.

**Is there a simpler tool that gives the exact answer?** A calculator, a spreadsheet, or a search
engine is exact, fast, and free, and gives the same answer every time. For the same sum over 10,000
rows, write the formula once. A model can help you write it.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

**Would a likely-sounding answer be a good one?** Rewording, summarizing, drafting, translating, and
brainstorming ask for text that reads well, which is exactly what the model predicts. A product, a
date, or a name asks for one exact answer, and the likely-looking one may not be it
(<ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />). Without a calculator, the model in <ChapterRef to="tools" /> worked out 1234 × 5678 step by
step and got 7,050,952. The answer is 7,006,652. The fix is a tool, or doing the exact part in code.

**Can you give it everything it needs to know?** A model knows only its prompt and what training left
in its weights: nothing after its cutoff, nothing about your files or last week's meeting, and little
about rare topics. Asked ten times who wrote *The Lanterns of Velmora*, a novel made up for this
site, it named nine different authors (<ChapterRef to="making-things-up" />). Put the facts in the prompt, or have the
harness search for them (<ChapterRef to="search-and-memory" />). In <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />, once the first exchange was dropped,
the model couldn't say which child loved the cat rhyme; with the exchange retrieved, it said Lily.

**Can you check the result quickly, and live with a mistake?** The model sounds just as sure when
it's wrong. Asked ten times who won the 1987 Tour de France, it said Eddy Merckx every time; it was
Stephen Roche. In <ChapterRef to="routing" />, the small model was 79% sure that Sydney is Australia's capital. So a
model saves time only when checking its work is quicker than doing the job yourself. A reworded
email takes seconds to read; a summary of a report you haven't read is hard to check without
reading the report; a contract clause needs a lawyer. Where mistakes are costly, keep a person in
the loop (<ChapterRef to="trust-and-guardrails" />), and test before you rely on it (<ChapterRef to="testing-agents" />).

**Is there a simpler tool that gives the exact answer?** A calculator, a spreadsheet formula, a
database query, or a search engine is exact, fast, and free, and gives the same answer every time.
A model costs tokens on every call, and its answers can change from one call to the next
(<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). Even the short call in <ChapterRef to="end-to-end" /> had the model read a 400-token prompt, so
ten thousand rows would be four million tokens. Write the rule once instead; a model can help you
write it.

</At>

