## Use both

<At levels="simple">

The best setups mix the two. The model does the wording, a tool does the exact part, the app fetches
the facts, and checks catch the mistakes. That is what the apps in the Harnesses chapters do
(<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" />).

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The questions rarely say "never". More often they say "not on its own": the model does the wording,
a tool does the exact work (<ChapterRef to="tools" />), the harness fetches the facts (<ChapterRef to="search-and-memory" />), and checks catch
the mistakes (<ChapterRef to="testing-agents" />). That division of labor is what a harness is for
(<ChapterRef to="the-model-is-not-the-product" />).

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The checking rule, written down

Say doing a job yourself takes $T$. With a model, you spend $a$ asking, $c$ checking, and, with
probability $p$ that the result is wrong, $f$ fixing it. The model saves time when

$$a + c + p\,f < T$$

Drafting does well: $c$ is a quick read and $f$ a quick edit. An unread report does badly: $c$ is
close to $T$, since checking means reading it. High stakes make $f$ large: a wrong clause in a
contract costs far more than the time it took to write.

### Agreement isn't a check of truth

The consistency check in <ChapterRef to="making-things-up" /> asked each of 11 questions ten times and trusted the
most common answer only when 8 or more of the ten agreed. That check flagged 4 of the 5 wrong answers and none of the 6 right ones. It missed
the Tour de France: all ten answers agreed, and all were wrong. Agreement measures how sure the
model is, not whether it is right, so a check you can automate still needs a source of truth for
what matters.

### Who gains most

In the writing experiment, the gap between the stronger and weaker writers shrank: those who scored
lower on their own gained the most from the help.<Cite id="noy2023" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A model writes likely text from its prompt and its training, so it suits jobs where likely text
  is good text.
- Exact answers, facts it wasn't given, and results you can't check are where it goes wrong.
- It saves time only when checking its work is quicker than doing the job yourself.
- A calculator, a formula, or a search beats it on exact, repeated work; a model can help write
  them.

<At levels="deep">

- Worth it when asking, checking, and the expected cost of fixing add up to less than doing it
  yourself: $a + c + p\,f < T$.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** if a model is the right tool, which one? There are hundreds to choose from.
